A screen shows my number.
The number is fifty-nine.
I walk up to the counter.
A man sits behind the counter.
"Good morning," he says.
"Good morning," I reply.
"My name is Maya, and I am thirty-one years old," I say.
"Welcome, Maya," he says.
"Do you have your passport?" he asks.
"Yes, I have my passport and my papers," I answer.
"Here you go," I say.
He looks at the papers.
He types on his computer.
I get a Danish personal identification number.
The number is private and very important.
He gives me a new card.
"Here you go, here is your card," he says.
"Now you are registered in Denmark."
I smile and take the card.
Now I am a part of Denmark.
